 "history": "The capot was the Canadian winter coat, issued to the militia and to the colonial regulars for campaigns: cut to overlap in front, the hood and collar often of another colour (d'Aleyrac), and worn with a sash, mitasses and moccasins; settlers and rangers wore it in winter too. Striped blanket capots were discouraged for the 1750s; plain cloth is the default, stripes an option [S2][S1][S3][M5][M7].",
